    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/EMAPA_29808 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/EMAPA_29808">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/EMAPA_35712"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000050"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/EMAPA_35710"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The dorsal prostate gland (dprgl) (TS:28). Prostate glands are subdivided into four bilaterally symmetrical regions (known as lobes in the human); anterior, dorsal, lateral and ventral prostate gland. The regions have different morphology and gene expression profiles which are specified during development via both common and region-specific genetic pathways. The 3D anatomy of the prostate gland is extremely complex.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>anatomical_structure</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>EMAPA:29808</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>dorsal prostate gland</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/EMAPA_29808"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>The dorsal prostate gland (dprgl) (TS:28). Prostate glands are subdivided into four bilaterally symmetrical regions (known as lobes in the human); anterior, dorsal, lateral and ventral prostate gland. The regions have different morphology and gene expression profiles which are specified during development via both common and region-specific genetic pathways. The 3D anatomy of the prostate gland is extremely complex.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:25968320</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


