    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/EMAPA_30467 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/EMAPA_30467">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/EMAPA_35963"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000050"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/EMAPA_17956"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002493"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/TS_24"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The epithelium of phallic urethra of male (ephur) (TS:20-TS:24) is derived from cloacal endoderm and derived from the urethral plate epithelium (TS:19). Located within the genital tubercle of male. At TS:20, the urethral plate epithelium has not canalised and only the plate is seen. At TS:21, as the plate begins to canalise and form a tube, it is subdivided into the urethral plate epithelium of male (syn: urethral plate), the bilaminar epithelium located distally, and into the phallic urethral tube epithelium (syn: proximal phallic urethral epithelium) which is the part of the urethral epithelium that has septated into a canalised tube proximally, and it is open at the proximal urethral meatus. The proximal urethral meatus closes in males, a process which begins around TS:23 and is not present in males at TS:25. In females, the proximal urethral meatus remains open to TS:27 (closed by TS:28, P8). The epithelium of phallic urethra of male/female becomes the epithelium of penile urethra/clitoral urethra at TS:25.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasAlternativeId>EMAPA:28853</oboInOwl:hasAlternativeId>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>anatomical_structure</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>EMAPA:30467</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>epithelium of phallic urethra of male</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/EMAPA_30467"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>The epithelium of phallic urethra of male (ephur) (TS:20-TS:24) is derived from cloacal endoderm and derived from the urethral plate epithelium (TS:19). Located within the genital tubercle of male. At TS:20, the urethral plate epithelium has not canalised and only the plate is seen. At TS:21, as the plate begins to canalise and form a tube, it is subdivided into the urethral plate epithelium of male (syn: urethral plate), the bilaminar epithelium located distally, and into the phallic urethral tube epithelium (syn: proximal phallic urethral epithelium) which is the part of the urethral epithelium that has septated into a canalised tube proximally, and it is open at the proximal urethral meatus. The proximal urethral meatus closes in males, a process which begins around TS:23 and is not present in males at TS:25. In females, the proximal urethral meatus remains open to TS:27 (closed by TS:28, P8). The epithelium of phallic urethra of male/female becomes the epithelium of penile urethra/clitoral urethra at TS:25.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:25968320</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/EMAPA_30470 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/EMAPA_30470">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/EMAPA_35962"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000050"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/EMAPA_36433"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002493"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/TS_24"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The epithelium of phallic urethra of female (ephur) (TS:20-TS:24) is derived from cloacal endoderm and derived from the urethral plate epithelium (TS:19). Located within the genital tubercle of male/female. At TS:20, the urethral plate epithelium has not canalised and only the plate is seen. At TS:21, as the plate begins to canalise and form a tube, it is subdivided into the urethral plate epithelium of male/female (syn: urethral plate), the bilaminar epithelium located distally, and into the phallic urethral tube epithelium (syn: proximal phallic urethral epithelium) which is the part of the urethral epithelium that has septated into a canalised tube proximally, and it is open at the proximal urethral meatus. The proximal urethral meatus closes in males, a process which begins around TS:23 and is not present in males at TS:25. In females, the proximal urethral meatus remains open to TS:27 (closed by TS:28, p8). The epithelium of phallic urethra of male/female becomes the epithelium of penile urethra/clitoral urethra at TS:25.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>anatomical_structure</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>EMAPA:30470</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>epithelium of phallic urethra of female</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/EMAPA_30470"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>The epithelium of phallic urethra of female (ephur) (TS:20-TS:24) is derived from cloacal endoderm and derived from the urethral plate epithelium (TS:19). Located within the genital tubercle of male/female. At TS:20, the urethral plate epithelium has not canalised and only the plate is seen. At TS:21, as the plate begins to canalise and form a tube, it is subdivided into the urethral plate epithelium of male/female (syn: urethral plate), the bilaminar epithelium located distally, and into the phallic urethral tube epithelium (syn: proximal phallic urethral epithelium) which is the part of the urethral epithelium that has septated into a canalised tube proximally, and it is open at the proximal urethral meatus. The proximal urethral meatus closes in males, a process which begins around TS:23 and is not present in males at TS:25. In females, the proximal urethral meatus remains open to TS:27 (closed by TS:28, p8). The epithelium of phallic urethra of male/female becomes the epithelium of penile urethra/clitoral urethra at TS:25.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:25968320</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/EMAPA_30478 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/EMAPA_30478">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/EMAPA_35702"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000050"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/EMAPA_18680"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The preputial swelling of female (pps) (syn: preputial fold of female, early prepuce of female) (TS:21-TS:23). Two lateral swellings develop on the sides of the genital tubercle at TS:21 and grow medially and ventrally. These swellings enclose the preputial glands. Comprised of skin of female preputial swelling, preputial gland of female, and mesenchyme of female preputial swelling.  At TS:24, when the two preputial swellings join/fuse together at the ventral midline of the genital tubercle of female, they become the prepuce of female.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>anatomical_structure</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>early prepuce of female</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>preputial fold of female</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:id>EMAPA:30478</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>preputial swelling of female</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/EMAPA_30478"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>The preputial swelling of female (pps) (syn: preputial fold of female, early prepuce of female) (TS:21-TS:23). Two lateral swellings develop on the sides of the genital tubercle at TS:21 and grow medially and ventrally. These swellings enclose the preputial glands. Comprised of skin of female preputial swelling, preputial gland of female, and mesenchyme of female preputial swelling.  At TS:24, when the two preputial swellings join/fuse together at the ventral midline of the genital tubercle of female, they become the prepuce of female.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:25968320</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


