    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/EMAPA_30901 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/EMAPA_30901">
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000050"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/EMAPA_36670"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002489"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/TS_21"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002493"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/TS_28"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The urethra (TS:21-TS:28) links the bladder/primitive bladder to the outside of the embryo. At TS:21 it is part of the urinary system. The urethra becomes sex-specific from TS:23. It is subdivided into pelvic urethra (located within the body of the embryo) and phallic urethra of male/female (located within the genital tubercle). Pelvic urethra is derived from the caudal urogenital sinus and phallic urethra is derived from urethral plate epithelium.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasAlternativeId>EMAPA:30891</oboInOwl:hasAlternativeId>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>anatomical_structure</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>EMAPA:30901</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>urethra</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/EMAPA_30901"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>The urethra (TS:21-TS:28) links the bladder/primitive bladder to the outside of the embryo. At TS:21 it is part of the urinary system. The urethra becomes sex-specific from TS:23. It is subdivided into pelvic urethra (located within the body of the embryo) and phallic urethra of male/female (located within the genital tubercle). Pelvic urethra is derived from the caudal urogenital sinus and phallic urethra is derived from urethral plate epithelium.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:25968320</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


